<div class="bg-zinc-950 text-zinc-100">
  <.marketing_nav current_user={@current_user} current={:docs} />

  <section class="py-12 sm:py-16">
    <.docs_layout
      current="runs"
      updated="August 4, 2026"
      toc={[
        {"the-list", "The Runs list"},
        {"statuses", "Run statuses"},
        {"run-detail", "A single run"},
        {"output", "Live output and caps"},
        {"cancelling", "Cancelling a run"},
        {"operations", "Operations and retries"}
      ]}
    >
      <.docs_header current="runs">
        <:dek>
          Use the Runs page to see what your fleet did. It lists every action dispatched to a host,
          with its requester, result, and policy decision.
        </:dek>
      </.docs_header>

      <.docs_h2 id="the-list">The Runs list</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Each row shows one invocation against one host. It shows the time, action ID, target runner,
        dispatcher, status, and duration. Rows update as a run moves through its lifecycle. You can
        watch a dispatch move from queued to finished without reloading. On a phone, the first line
        shows the action and status. The second line shows the origin, runner, and time.
      </p>
      <.docs_screenshot
        src="/images/screenshots/runs.webp"
        title="emisar console · Runs"
        caption="Every dispatch with who asked for it, where it ran, and a color-coded outcome."
        alt="The Runs list: columns for When, Action, Runner, Dispatched by, Status, and Duration. Rows show actions like caddy.reload_config and postgres.replication_lag dispatched across runners edge-fra-01, api-iad-02, and pg-primary-iad, each attributed to a named operator or agent, with color-coded statuses — awaiting approval, success, cancelled, and failed — and a per-run duration."
      />
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Four filters narrow the feed. Each one lives in the URL. The back button, a refresh,
        and a shared link all land on the same view:
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Status.</strong>
            One or more lifecycle states. You can pull up just the failures, just what is awaiting
            approval, or just what is still in flight.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Action.</strong>
            A substring match on the action id. Typing
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">postgres</code>
            finds <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">postgres.vacuum</code>
            and <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">postgres.uptime</code>
            — and
            it is case-insensitive.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Runner.</strong>
            A searchable list of your runners, to scope the feed to one host.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Dispatched by.</strong>
            The origin: Operator, AI agent, or Runbook. Select an origin to show a second filter.
            Use that filter to choose a team member, agent, or runbook.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        A runner's page links straight to its own runs. An agent's page links to the runs
        dispatched with its key. The common "what did this host, or this agent, do" question is
        one filtered link, not a hand-built query.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="statuses">Run statuses</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        A run moves through a small state machine. It starts <em>pending</em>, becomes <em>sent</em>, then <em>running</em>. From there it settles on exactly one
        terminal outcome. Policy can settle a run before it ever reaches a host. A
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/policies-and-approvals"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >require-approval decision</.link>
        parks it at <em>pending approval</em>, and a deny decision
        ends it at <em>denied</em>.
      </p>
      <div class="my-6 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-zinc-900">
        <table class="w-full min-w-[680px] text-left text-sm">
          <thead class="border-b border-zinc-900 text-xs uppercase tracking-wider text-zinc-400">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Status</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">What it means</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-zinc-900 text-zinc-400">
            <%= for {status, meaning} <- EmisarWeb.RunStatuses.all() do %>
              <tr>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top whitespace-nowrap font-medium text-zinc-200">
                  {status}
                </td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top">{meaning}</td>
              </tr>
            <% end %>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Three terminal statuses look similar but require different responses.
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">Denied</strong>
        requires a policy change. <strong class="text-zinc-200">Refused</strong>
        usually requires pack trust or signing repair.
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">Failed</strong>
        is a real action result, not a gate.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="run-detail">A single run</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Open a row from
        <a
          href={~p"/app/runs"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Runs</a>
        to see one invocation. The fact strip starts with the outcome. It shows <strong>Status</strong>, <strong>Runner</strong>, <strong>Dispatched by</strong>, <strong>Duration</strong>, <strong>Exit code</strong>, and
        <strong>Started</strong>
        time. The
        Runner value links to that host.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <strong>Dispatched by</strong>
        names the accountable person first. An operator run shows the team member who ran it. An
        agent run shows the human who owns the agent key. It also shows the client as secondary
        context: <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">jordan@example.com via Claude Code</code>. A
        runbook run names the runbook. This is the same human-first
        attribution the audit trail uses, so "who ran this, and through what" is answered on the row
        itself.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        A <strong>Why</strong>
        section carries the justification. Every dispatch includes a
        <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">reason</code>
        from the operator or agent. An agent can also include evidence and expected outcome.
        Evidence records what it observed. Expected records the outcome that confirms success. When
        policy intervenes, the run shows the matching rules and exact policy version. A routine
        allow is omitted because every dispatched run already passed policy.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Below that, <strong>Arguments</strong>
        shows the values passed to the action. It includes a
        <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">sha256</code>
        fingerprint of the exact bytes. The control plane receives and stores the exact arguments.
        It uses them for authorization, approval, dispatch, and hashing. UI and API views mask
        fields that the action marks as sensitive. Secrets in unmarked or unknown fields remain in
        the stored arguments. <strong>Executed command</strong>
        shows the shell line that the runner ran. The runner applies configured redaction to the
        command and output before egress. This is a best-effort backstop, not a guarantee. See
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/security-model"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >security model</.link>
        for how the redaction pipeline works.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="output">Live output and caps</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The output panel streams stdout and stderr line by line as the runner produces them. The
        stderr stream uses a separate tint to highlight errors. A <em>streaming</em>
        marker appears while the run is active. A run with no output says so instead of showing an
        empty terminal.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The terminal renders the most recent 500 chunks. A run that streamed more shows a note that
        earlier output was trimmed. This is a
        display limit that keeps a chatty run from growing the page without bound. It caps what is on
        screen, not what the runner sent or what is recorded.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Truncation can also happen at the source. The runner bounds the output it captures per the
        action's output settings. When it hits that limit it clips the stream and flags the run as
        truncated. Each run also records whether every progress chunk arrived. A gap left by a
        mid-run disconnect is visible rather than silent.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Every run carries a durable output budget. Up to 50,000 progress chunks and 64 MiB in total,
        with any single chunk capped at 256 KiB. A run that will exceed the budget stops accepting
        further chunks. The cap is charged under the run's row lock. It holds across runner
        reconnects. A runaway or hostile runner cannot flood the control plane by streaming without
        end.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        To watch a runner from the host, tail its service log and local event journal over SSH:
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="shell"><span class="text-zinc-500"># the runner's service log and its local event journal, live</span>
<span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> journalctl -u emisar -f
<span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> sudo emisar events tail -f</.docs_code>
      <.docs_callout
        kind={:note}
        title="The Runs page is your live view; the audit trail is the record."
      >
        Run output and events follow your plan's retention window. The audit trail exports terminal
        outcomes, denials, approval gates, and approval decisions. Pending, sent, and running state
        remains in run history. See the
        <.link navigate={~p"/docs/audit-and-siem"}>audit trail</.link>
        and export events before the retention window closes.
      </.docs_callout>

      <.docs_h2 id="cancelling">Cancelling a run</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        You can stop a run that has not finished. Open it from
        <a
          href={~p"/app/runs"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Runs</a>
        and use <strong>Cancel run</strong>
        —
        available while the run is pending, sent, or running, to operators and above. What happens
        depends on where the run is:
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Queued (pending).</strong>
            The run has not reached the runner yet. It is cancelled immediately and never
            dispatches.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">In flight (sent or running).</strong>
            The control plane signals the runner with SIGTERM, then SIGKILL if needed. The run
            remains at <em>cancelling</em>
            until the runner reports its final outcome. The runner remains the authority on what
            stopped.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Cancellation is recorded in the audit trail and is idempotent. Asking to cancel a run that
        already finished does nothing. A run that is <em>awaiting approval</em>
        is not cancelled here. Deny its approval instead. This pulls the run back and records the
        denial reason. See <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/policies-and-approvals"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >policies and approvals</.link>.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="operations">Operations and retries</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Each run records one action invocation. Running the action again from a runner page,
        runbook, or agent creates a new run. emisar never retries a finished run without a new
        request. An in-flight run survives a runner reconnect. A queued run dispatches after its
        runner returns. See
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/runner-fleet"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >the runner fleet</.link>
        guide for reconnect and stuck-run behavior.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        When one dispatch targets several hosts, each host gets its own run. This occurs when an
        agent sends an action to a runner group over MCP. emisar groups those runs under one
        operation. A fan-out supports up to 16 runners and returns one result per host. Replaying
        the same operation returns the original runs instead of dispatching duplicates. A lost
        response does not cause a second execution.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        A runbook works the same way. One execution groups every run its steps produced, in dispatch
        order. A multi-step sequence reads as one unit rather than a scatter of unrelated rows. See <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/runbooks"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >runbooks</.link>. For
        recovering an exact operation after an ambiguous response, the
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/mcp-reference"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >MCP CLI &amp; reference</.link>
        covers the idempotency and recovery contract.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        A run that failed, timed out, or looks stuck —
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/troubleshooting" <> "#runs"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >Troubleshooting</.link>
        starts from the symptom.
      </p>
    </.docs_layout>
  </section>

  <.marketing_footer />
</div>
